# Product Roadmap — orientation

This is a **product strategy home**, written and read by the Human-Loop skills. It is
read-me-first: it explains the taxonomy and the folder conventions so that anyone (human or
agent) browsing the directory tree understands what each part *means*. It holds *why we build,
how we'll know it worked, and the work in flight* — above any one repo.

- `/roadmap-init` scaffolds these conventions (once); `/roadmap` writes the strategy (visions, bets, metrics).
- `/evaluate-outcome` reads a bet and appends its metric readings + verdict.
- `/intent` reads a bet or backlog item to ground a PRD's *why*, and **registers the PRD it
  creates back** onto that backlog item (tying output to outcome).
- `/evaluate-pr` may **file a follow-up** (a feature or bug found during review) into the backlog.

The build harness never builds from this home — it lives outside any `prds/` and carries no
sentinels.

This file is **static conventions, written once.** It does **not** track which visions, bets,
or backlog items exist — the **folder structure is the map.** To see what's here, list the
directories (or query the frontmatter; see "This is an Obsidian vault" below).

## The taxonomy (narrative → number, top to bottom)

| Level | What it is | Where it lives | Metric |
|---|---|---|---|
| **Vision** | Narrative direction — where the product is going and why. Prose, not a number. | `<vision>/roadmap.md` | — |
| **Strategic intent** | A chosen *focus area* + its **lagging** outcome (the slow metric: retention, revenue, churn) + why it's the priority now. One per vision (up to ~three for a large org). | `<vision>/roadmap.md` | lagging |
| **Bet (initiative)** | A *problem*, framed as a falsifiable hypothesis with an expected value, that moves a **leading** indicator believed to feed the intent's lagging outcome. | `<vision>/bets/<bet>/bet.md` | leading |
| **Backlog item** | A unit of work with **no** hypothesis and **no** metric: a **feature** (new capability) or a **bugfix**. Optionally serves a bet (`bet:` frontmatter). `/intent` turns one into a PRD. | `<vision>/backlog/<type>/<slug>.md` | — |
| **Metric** | An instrument that reads a number from the live world, plus its append-only readings (baseline = line 0). | `<vision>/metrics/<metric>/` | — |
| **PRD (option)** | A *solution* slice — the downstream, buildable form of a feature backlog item. Built by the harness, verified by a runner. | a code repo's `prds/<feature>/` | — (tests *built*, never *moved*) |

**The line that must not blur:** a PRD's runner checks *built* (deterministic, pre-merge);
a bet's metric checks *moved* (lagging, post-merge, advisory). The metric lives on the
**bet**, never in a runner — and never on a backlog item.

## What "done" means here — read before you reason about progress

A **bet** is a learning instrument, not a checklist. The features and bugfixes in its backlog
are **options** — attempts to move the bet's leading indicator. Shipping all of them does
**not** mean the bet is done, or that it worked.

A bet is **done only when its metric is read** and you can say which way it moved. Shipping
every option with the indicator flat is **not** a failure to finish — it *is* the learning,
and that learning is the input to the next bet.

So never gauge a bet by how much of its backlog has shipped — that's the **build trap**:
mistaking output for outcome. Track output to know what's been *tried*; read the **outcome**
to know if it *worked*.

## Folder layout — what each path means

```
<home>/                              # this folder (repo root if multi-repo product, else roadmap/)
  AGENTS.md                          # this file — the conventions, written once
  <vision-name>/                     # ONE per vision. The folder name IS the vision slug.
    roadmap.md                       # vision (narrative) + strategic intent + lagging outcome + candidate bets
    bets/
      <bet-slug>/                    # ONE per bet. Folder name = the bet slug.
        bet.md                       # problem, hypothesis, expected value, leading metric, horizon, status, verdict
    backlog/                         # the unified work backlog (features + bugfixes)
      AGENTS.md                      # how to write a backlog item — read before adding one
      feature/
        <slug>.md                    # a feature backlog item (new capability); one file per item
      bugfix/
        <slug>.md                    # a bugfix backlog item; one file per item
    metrics/
      <metric-slug>/                 # ONE per metric. Folder name = the metric slug.
        measure-outcome.sh           # the probe: reads the metric from the live world
        query.sql | rubric.md        # what the probe reads (instrument-dependent)
        readings.log                 # append-only time-series; line 0 is the baseline
```

So: a sub-folder of `<home>/` is a **vision**; under it, each folder in `bets/` is a
**bet**, each file in `backlog/<type>/` is a **backlog item**, and each folder in `metrics/`
is a **metric**. There is no index to keep in sync — read the tree (or query frontmatter).

## This is an Obsidian vault

This home is read **in Obsidian**. Every note carries that weight; treat keeping it tidy as
part of the work, not an afterthought:

- **Frontmatter on every note.** YAML at the top (`type`, `status`, `bet`, `repos`, `created`,
  …). The per-artifact conventions live in the sibling `AGENTS.md` (e.g. `backlog/AGENTS.md`).
- **`[[wikilinks]]` between artifacts.** Link a backlog item to the bet it serves
  (`bet: "[[<bet-slug>]]"`), a bet to its metric, a PRD pointer back to its item. The links
  are the graph; keep them live.
- **Dataview rollups, not maintained indexes.** "All work for bet X" is a *query* over
  `bet:` frontmatter — not a hand-kept list, and not a folder (backlog items are flat peers).
  `roadmap.md` surfaces a bet's in-flight work with a Dataview block; the folder tree stays
  the source of truth.

Maintaining frontmatter and links is **every agent's job here** — if you add or change an
artifact, update its frontmatter and its `[[links]]` so the graph and the queries stay true.
(Raw GitHub renders `[[links]]` and Dataview weakly; this vault is meant to be opened in
Obsidian. Keep frontmatter human-legible regardless.)

## When to start a NEW vision, a new bet, or a backlog item (your call; this is the coaching)
- **New bet** — a fresh problem with a *falsifiable hypothesis and a metric to move*, under an
  *existing* strategic intent. Add a folder under the existing `<vision>/bets/`.
- **New vision** — a *different* strategic intent: a different big lagging focus. Add a new
  `<vision-name>/` folder.
- **New backlog item** — necessary work with **no** new hypothesis or metric: a **feature**
  (new capability) or a **bugfix**. It may *serve* a bet (link it) or stand alone. Add a file
  under `<vision>/backlog/feature/` or `backlog/bugfix/` (see `backlog/AGENTS.md`).
- Resist **peanut-buttering** — don't spin up a vision per idea. Most new ideas are bets or
  backlog items. The fork is *your* decision; `/roadmap` coaches, never auto-forks.

## How to read this home
List `<home>/` to see the visions → open a `<vision>/roadmap.md` for the vision + lagging
anchor + candidate bets → open a `<vision>/bets/<bet>/bet.md` for the initiative + its leading
metric → browse `<vision>/backlog/` (or query `bet:` frontmatter) for the work serving it →
look in `<vision>/metrics/<metric>/` for the probe + readings.
